<p>British police arrested three men on March 4 on suspicion of assisting China’s foreign intelligence service, an offense under the UK’s National Security Act 2023. Police also searched several properties as part of the investigation. The case has drawn extra attention after UK media reported that one of those held is the spouse of a sitting Labour lawmaker, and another is the spouse of a former Labour MP. Joani Reid, the Labour MP for East Kilbride and Strathaven, said she had never seen anything to make her suspect her husband had broken the law, while stressing she and her children are not part of the investigation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DlORS7m39FC1_-qio5fILwGblPRlzxDiNgdgnI8NeW7SG9tG87tu_X7VlHKuByBR8YhEAmq6d6mlObgJXwrVVf_8t7zKujkjSyOdk7Avnvw1xZNH7ZlrNMKLP73qGjb6wht1vZHkSsBljSpi-WVEDi53iyjZHUJb8IRmog-WniGiqphHp54zyyzfFwQumcCjPucxhCRBvH8GnQ' alt='A Chinese embassy building in London in November. Chinese spying has been a sensitive topic in Britain recently.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Arrest details: Police arrested a 39-year-old in London, and men aged 68 and 43 in Powys and Pontyclun in Wales; all three remain in custody.</li><li>Search locations: Officers searched properties in London, Cardiff and East Kilbride as the investigation continued.</li><li>Parliament mention: Conservative MP Alex Burghart told parliament that the Guardian and the BBC had reported one suspect was the spouse of a sitting Labour MP and another was the spouse of a former Labour MP.</li><li>China’s reaction: China’s embassy in London condemned the allegations, saying some are trying to “fabricate facts and concoct so-called ‘espionage cases’”.</li><li>Diplomatic timing: The arrests came weeks after UK Prime Minister Keir Starmer traveled to Beijing as the government worked to repair ties with China.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Metropolitan Police (Counter Terrorism Command): Police say the men are suspected of assisting a foreign intelligence service under the National Security Act 2023. They have not been named because they have not been charged. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-04-0800/china-london-police-arrest-3-men-suspicion-spying-china-one-uk-lawmakers-husband'>AllSides (via reporting on Metropolitan Police statement)</a>)</li><li>Joani Reid, Labour MP: Reid said she has not seen anything suggesting her husband broke the law and emphasized that she and her children are not part of the investigation. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/04/world/europe/uk-spying-china-arrests.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Chinese embassy in London: The embassy criticized the case as an attempt to “fabricate facts and concoct so-called ‘espionage cases’.” (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978510/spouses-of-uk-mps-among-three-held-on-suspicion-of-spying-for-china'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/uk-lawmaker-s-partner-arrested-over-china-spying-allegations/a-76220229'>UK lawmaker&#x27;s partner arrested over China spying allegations</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1978510/spouses-of-uk-mps-among-three-held-on-suspicion-of-spying-for-china'>Spouses of UK MPs among three held on suspicion of spying for China</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/british-police-arrest-lawmakers-husband-on-suspicion-of-spying-for-china-13986364.html'>British police arrest lawmaker’s husband on suspicion of spying for China</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-03-04-0800/china-london-police-arrest-3-men-suspicion-spying-china-one-uk-lawmakers-husband'>London police arrest 3 men on suspicion of spying for China. One is a UK lawmaker&#x27;s husband</a> - allsides.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/04/world/europe/uk-spying-china-arrests.html'>Britain Arrests a Lawmaker’s Husband on Suspicion of Spying for China</a> - nytimes.com</li></ul>